On Sat, 10 Oct 2026 11:19:42 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> I think this is reasonable. However, I want determinism wherever
> possible. So we should deterministically fetch (and maybe rebase?) and
> then call into the model to resolve any conflicts if it needs to.

Posted as "[PATCH 0/2] Get up to date deterministically before
process-email" [1]. It adds libexec/mail/get-up-to-date.sh, run by
just get-up-to-date. The script does the fetch and both rebases.

It rebases too, because a rebase needs no judgement under jj. A
conflicting rebase still succeeds and records the conflict in the
commit, so the script never stops partway and leaves a half-done
tree. The steps are:

- fetch every remote;
- rebase my unmerged trunk work onto trunk@upstream;
- rebase my mail outbox onto mail@upstream, or point it at the list's
  tip if nothing is pending;
- start a fresh mail change and run notmuch new.

The script exits non-zero only when a step can't run at all, such as
a fetch that can't reach a remote. The loop then stops.

> As a principle, we should:
> - run deterministic code where possible
> - run model inference where necessary
> - give the model access to run the same tools deterministically

The series follows that split. just process-email runs the recipe
before each lookup, so it picks the next message from the current
list, and an empty local index no longer ends the loop early. The
skill's step 1 runs the same recipe and keeps for the model only the
work that needs judgement: resolving conflicted commits
(conflicts() & mine()), and abandoning patches you applied with
edits, which the rebase can't recognize as landed.

> In other words, I wouldn't want to only do the fetch and rebase in the
> prompt. There should be a deterministic "best effort" get up to date
> script - and then inference to resolve any stuff that will take more
> work.

The prompt no longer contains the fetch or rebase commands. I ran the
script twice on my tree; the second run changed nothing and exited 0.

The series builds on "[PATCH 0/3] Add just process-email" [2], which
adds the loop it changes, so it should go in after that one.

